Paragraph 6 of Part II of Fifth Schedule
Requirements relating to
medical insurance for day
surgery and in-patient care, etc.
Definitions
1. In this Schedule —
“controlled drug” has the meaning given by section 2 of the Misuse of Drugs Act 1973;
“hospitalisation discharge date”, in relation to any insured day surgery or insured in-patient care received by an insured foreign employee, means a date on which the insured foreign employee is —
(a)certified, by a medical practitioner treating the insured foreign employee, to be medically fit for discharge from the insured day surgery or insured in-patient care, as the case may be; and
(b)assessed by that medical practitioner to have a feasible discharge option;
“insured day surgery” means any kind of day surgery covered by the insurance cover in accordance with this Schedule;
“insured in-patient care” means any kind of in-patient care covered by the insurance cover in accordance with this Schedule;
“insured related out-patient treatment”, in relation to an insured foreign employee, means all or any of the following:
(a)any related out-patient care that is received by the insured foreign employee within a period of 7 calendar days starting on his or her hospitalisation discharge date;
(b)any related out-patient rehabilitation treatment that is prescribed by a medical practitioner during the insured day surgery or insured in-patient care received by the insured foreign employee, and is received by the insured foreign employee at any time after his or her hospitalisation discharge date;
“rehabilitation treatment” means any physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy or any other type of rehabilitation treatment;
“related out-patient care”, in relation to an insured foreign employee, means any treatment, service or item (other than any related out-patient rehabilitation treatment) that —
(a)arises from any insured day surgery or insured in-patient care received by the insured foreign employee; and
(b)is received by him or her as an out-patient;
“related out-patient rehabilitation treatment”, in relation to an insured foreign employee, means any rehabilitation treatment that —
(a)arises from any insured day surgery or insured in-patient care received by the insured foreign employee; and
(b)is received by him or her as an out-patient.
[S 436/2025 wef 01/07/2025]
Limits and sub-limits on claims
2.—(1) Subject to sub-paragraph (2), the limit on the total amount of claims payable by the insurer under the insurance cover must be at least $60,000 for all of the following medical costs (called in this Schedule the relevant medical costs):
(a)costs of insured day surgery and insured in-patient care received by the insured foreign employee;
(b)costs of insured related out-patient treatment received by the insured foreign employee.
(2) Where the insurance cover provides for sub-limits for different types of claims in relation to the relevant medical costs, the limit on the total amount payable by the insurer under each sub-limit must be at least $60,000.
[S 436/2025 wef 01/07/2025]
First dollar coverage amount
3. The insurance cover must provide for an amount of at least the first $15,000 in aggregate of claims for the relevant medical costs to be payable in full by an insurer (called in this Schedule the first dollar coverage amount), before there is any requirement under the insurance cover for —
(a)the deduction of a deductible; or
(b)any co-insurance of the claims, by the employer and the insurer.
[S 436/2025 wef 01/07/2025]
[S 875/2025 wef 01/01/2026]
Co-insurance of claim
4. Where the insurance cover requires co-insurance of a claim by the employer and the insurer for the relevant medical costs, the insurance cover must require the insurer to pay an amount for the claim equivalent to —
(a)at least 75% of the amount of the relevant medical costs in excess of —
(i)the first dollar coverage amount claimed under the insurance cover; and
(ii)the amount deducted as a deductible under the insurance cover; or
(b)the balance remaining from the limit on the total amount of claims (in accordance with paragraph 2) after the payment of prior claims under the insurance cover, if the amount of the balance remaining is less than the amount payable under sub-paragraph (a).
[S 436/2025 wef 01/07/2025]
[S 875/2025 wef 01/01/2026]
Permissible exclusions to insurance cover
5. Exclusions to the insurance cover (if any) for the insured foreign employee may only be in relation to all or any of the following matters:
(a)treatment for or in respect of any birth defect, congenital abnormality or hereditary condition suffered by the insured foreign employee, including any related complication from that treatment;
(b)treatment (not being any treatment mentioned in sub-paragraph (a)) within the first 12 months of employment with the same employer, for or in respect of any pre-existing medical condition that is known to the employer or the foreign employee before the commencement of the insurance cover for that foreign employee;
(c)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from any malicious, unlawful or wilful act of —
(i)the employer of the insured foreign employee; or
(ii)where the insured foreign employee is a domestic worker — any family member of the employer;
(d)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from any criminal act of the insured foreign employee;
(e)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from the insured foreign employee’s voluntary participation in any hazardous sport, including any winter sport (such as skiing or snowboarding), underwater activity (such as snorkelling or scuba diving), aerial activity (such as taking a helicopter tour or para-gliding) or motor sport (such as motorcycle racing or motor car racing);
(f)treatment for or in respect of pregnancy of the insured foreign employee, or childbirth (including any delivery procedure such as caesarean delivery) or abortion undergone by the insured foreign employee, including any related complication from that treatment;
(g)treatment for or in respect of any sexually-transmitted disease, AIDS (Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome) or ARC (AIDS Related Complex) suffered by the insured foreign employee;
(h)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from the insured foreign employee’s addiction to a controlled drug;
(i)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising directly or indirectly from any nuclear fallout;
(j)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury of the insured foreign employee arising from any war, war-like situation, civil war, mutiny, rebellion, revolution or act of terrorism;
(k)treatment for which the insured foreign employee received reimbursement under any insurance relating to work injury compensation required under the Work Injury Compensation Act 2019 or other forms of insurance coverage;
(l)treatment received by the insured foreign employee outside Singapore;
(m)the second or subsequent occasion of the treatment described in each of the following sub-paragraphs:
(i)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from —
(A)any attempted suicide by the insured foreign employee; or
(B)any self-inflicted injury by the insured foreign employee, or any attempt by the insured foreign employee to cause self-inflicted injury,
whether or not arising from the same attempted suicide, self-inflicted injury or attempted self-inflicted injury;
(ii)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from the insured foreign employee’s voluntary participation in any strike, riot or civil commotion, whether or not arising from the same strike, riot or civil commotion;
(iii)treatment for or in respect of any psychiatric or nervous or mental disorder suffered by the insured foreign employee;
(iv)treatment for or in respect of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee arising from the insured foreign employee’s addiction to alcohol, or to a drug that is not a controlled drug;
(n)any contraceptive operation or procedure for the insured foreign employee, including any related complication from that operation or procedure;
(o)any operation or procedure for or in respect of —
(i)any assisted conception for the insured foreign employee; or
(ii)any treatment of infertility or sub-fertility suffered by the insured foreign employee,
including any related complication from that operation or procedure;
(p)sex re-assignment surgery for the insured foreign employee, including any related complication from that surgery;
(q)dental treatment or service for the insured foreign employee that is not necessitated by any accident;
(r)any related out-patient care, or related out-patient rehabilitation treatment, received by the insured foreign employee, that does not fall within the definition of insured related out-patient treatment;
(s)ambulance fees;
(t)private nursing fees;
(u)any vaccination;
(v)any screening or test that is unrelated to any in-patient care or day surgery;
(w)treatment of a preventive nature;
(x)cosmetic surgery for self-beautification;
(y)medical repatriation;
(z)traditional, complementary or alternative medicine;
(za)purchase of medical equipment;
(zb)optional items such as television, telephone, special requested meals and other items which are not necessary for the treatment of any medical condition or injury suffered by the insured foreign employee.
[S 436/2025 wef 01/07/2025]
Option for direct claim by medical provider, etc.
6.—(1) The terms of the medical insurance policy must provide an option for a medical provider to submit a claim directly to the insurer.
(2) The insurance policy must require the insurer to pay the amount payable by the insurer under the insurance cover in accordance with this Schedule directly to the medical provider, on submission of a claim mentioned in sub-paragraph (1).
(3) In this paragraph —
“licensable healthcare service” has the meaning given by section 3(1) of the Healthcare Services Act 2020;
“medical provider” means a healthcare service provider who holds a licence under the Healthcare Services Act 2020 to provide any licensable healthcare service.
